<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Merged into the bundle's Info.plist by tauri-bundler.

    Local network access.

    Since macOS 15 an app must declare why it talks to the local network, and
    from 26 the enforcement is thorough: without this key a GUI-launched app is
    denied LAN traffic SILENTLY. No error, no prompt — sockets simply never
    reach anything. Every symptom looks like the device being absent.

    It does not show up in development because a process started from Terminal
    inherits Terminal's own permission. The app only fails once it is
    double-clicked, which is to say only for the people using it.

    This app proxies to an Analog Way LivePremier processor on the local network;
    without LAN access the processor is unreachable and the UI stays empty.

    Verify after a build with:
      plutil -p <app>.app/Contents/Info.plist | grep NSLocalNetwork
  -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>LivePremier Plus talks to an Analog Way LivePremier processor on your local network.</string>
  <!--
    Apple events to PowerPoint and Keynote.

    The Background Slicer turns a PowerPoint or Keynote deck into a PDF by
    asking PowerPoint or Keynote to export it over AppleScript
    (plugins/bg-slicer/convert.js). The first time for each, macOS asks
    whether this app may control it, and shows this sentence; without it a
    hardened-runtime app's request is refused with -1743 and no prompt at all. The matching entitlement is
    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events in entitlements.plist.
  -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>LivePremier Plus asks PowerPoint or Keynote to export a deck as a PDF, so its slides can be made into stills and backgrounds for your switcher.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
